notes.body:
Ok, I'll remove those 2 warnings  

I put them while developing as I encountered an issue which seems "to result in a random decryption"  

In fact, I think I put the finger on the root cause:  
It's due to the intrinsic nature of the PQ Hybrid key material, so SSH already transmit the whole blob as an `mpint`

So applying this part of code to PQ Hybrid KEMs result in something which could be seen as "a random decryption success"

```c
    // shared secret data needs to be written as an mpint, and we need it later
        if (secret->data[0] & 0x80) {         // Stored in Big endian
            length = secret->length + 1;
            char *tmp = (char *)wmem_alloc0(wmem_packet_scope(), length);
            memcpy(tmp + 1, secret->data, secret->length);
            tmp[0] = 0;
            secret->data = tmp;
            secret->length = length;
        }

```

Why it fails ?
Because Wireshark try to make an `mpint` from an already transmitted `mpint`when MSB is set which result in: 

SSH transmit:
```bash
encoded shared secret:
0000: 00 00 00 20 f4 bd 88 ca 8d b8 85 b2 fb 6c 42 8e  ... .........lB.
0016: 39 10 6e 87 68 7a 22 e7 9f 68 2f 58 0b 68 ba 35  9.n.hz"..h/X.h.5
0032: 60 48 ec f9                                      `H..
```


Wireshark do:
```bash
shared secret[37]
0000: 00 00 00 21 00 f4 bd 88 ca 8d b8 85 b2 fb 6c 42  ...!..........lB
0016: 8e 39 10 6e 87 68 7a 22 e7 9f 68 2f 58 0b 68 ba  .9.n.hz"..h/X.h.
0032: 35 60 48 ec f9                                   5`H..
```

And after all derivative keys are wrong and decryption failed 


I did apply a fix : 
```c
    // shared secret data needs to be written as an mpint, and we need it later
    if (kex_type == SSH_KEX_SNTRUP761X25519 || kex_type == SSH_KEX_MLKEM768X25519) {
        // For PQ KEMs: use shared_secret as-is, whether SHARED_SECRET or PRIVATE_KEY
        // Do NOT prepend 0x00 (OpenSSH already encodes correctly for PQ KEM)
        ssh_hash_buffer_put_string(global_data->kex_shared_secret, secret->data, secret->length);
    } else {
        // For all other KEX types (e.g., curve25519, ecdh-sha2, etc.)
        // Pad with 0x00 if MSB is set, to comply with mpint format (RFC 4251)
        if (secret->data[0] & 0x80) {         // Stored in Big endian
            length = secret->length + 1;
            char *tmp = (char *)wmem_alloc0(wmem_packet_scope(), length);
            memcpy(tmp + 1, secret->data, secret->length);
            tmp[0] = 0;
            secret->data = tmp;
            secret->length = length;
        }
        ssh_hash_buffer_put_string(global_data->kex_shared_secret, secret->data, secret->length);
    }
```

And now the decryption work really better, so I think we can remove warnings.

There is still a point to address: [malformed packets] on frame ~19 / ~20 : 

```bash
19	0.289889511	10.0.50.78	10.0.50.77	40742	22	140	SSHv2	Encrypted packet (plaintext_len=124), User Authentication Request[Malformed Packet]			50
20	0.292700514	10.0.50.77	10.0.50.78	22	40742	100	SSHv2	Encrypted packet (plaintext_len=84), Public Key algorithm accepted[Malformed Packet]			60

```

@johnthacker 

I already encountered another issue here when keylog file contains PRIVATE_KEY.

At the moment Wireshark don't have the required cryptographic functions to handle PQ Hybrid material

I need Lattice crypto (ntrup) + Kyber crypto (mlkem) to do something like:

```c
x25519_shared_secret = x25519(priv, peer_pub);
kem_shared_secret = sntrup761_dec(ciphertext, sntrup761_priv);
shared_secret = kem_shared_secret || x25519_shared_secret;
encoded_shared_secret = sha512(sntrup761_dec(ciphertext, sntrup761_priv) || x25519_shared_secret));

```  

Having a look at openssh-portable code show that's about more than 2000 lines of code only to handle sntrup761 (maybe the same for Kyber crypto too) so I'm not sure that I can easily add it to the dissector (it doesn't seems that's a good idea... to be honest for me it's a bad idea except if it's added as a library).

Any idea ?

thanks again for help  
